"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Festivals","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The second generation","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Spiritual biographies","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Personalities, perspectives, practices","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Lay Ordination","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Familiar themes","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The (re)emergence of a new theme.","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}}],"subject_place":["United States"],"covers":[8547313],"local_id":["urn:cst:10017040560"],"lc_classifications":["BQ734 .N86 1996","BQ734.N86 1996"],"key":"/books/OL1273014M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL657275A","name":"Paul David Numrich"}],"subtitle":"Americanization in two immigrant Theravada Buddhist temples","publish_places":["Knoxville"],"subjects":["Wat Dhammaram (Chicago) -- History.","Dharma Vijaya Buddhist Vihara (Los Angeles) -- History.","Theravāda Buddhism -- United States -- History -- 20th century."],"subject_time":["20th century."],"edition_name":"1st ed.","pagination":"xxiv, 181 p. :","source_records":["amazon:087049905X","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC1_barcode.mrc:216398812:4380","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part24.utf8:92668488:1572","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC1_multibarcode.mrc:216667063:4380","bwb:9780870499050","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-004.mrc:314548414:2983"],"title":"Old wisdom in the New World","dewey_decimal_class":["294.3/91/0973"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references (p. [155]-176) and index"},"number_of_pages":181,"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"lccn":["95004361"],"isbn_10":["087049905X"],"publish_date":"1996","publish_country":"tnu","by_statement":"Paul David Numrich.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL3744079W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"oclc_numbers":["32275970"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Focusing on two well-established institutions - one in Chicago, the other in Los Angeles - Old Wisdom in the New World is the first systematic examination of the growing presence of Theravada Buddhist temples in the United States.\n\nPaul David Numrich's socio-historical analysis highlights a number of classic Americanization themes of establishment, growth, and adaptation. These have surfaced, the author shows, in debates over the retention of Old World culture and language, the \"problem\" of the second generation, and the role of the laity in religious institutions.\n\nGoing beyond such familiar themes, Numrich also uncovers the intriguing phenomenon of ethnically defined \"parallel congregations\" in these temples, as he reveals the ways in which Asian-immigrant Buddhists and American converts pursue substantively different expressions of the Theravada tradition under the direction of a shared clerical leadership, the resident monks.\n\nIn the author's view, these Theravada case studies underline the complexity of the present Americanization process. By examining the intersection of two important trends - the steady growth of Asian immigration and an increasing indigenous interest in new religious movements, especially those of Asian origin - this book points to some fascinating new directions for the study of religious and cultural diversity in the United States."},"latest_revision":10,"revision":10,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2024-07-19T00:57:09.263034"}}},"ISBN:9780091069216":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9780091069216","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL5453344M/Mind_unshaken","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL5453344M/Mind_unshaken","thumbnail_url":"h